# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "The dashboard lists the configured servers on its MCP page. The configured set is proven at deploy time: the deploy runs ``hermes mcp test <server>`` for every configured server and fails when one does not answer. A successful run reports the negotiated connection and the discovered tool count. The Playwright spec covers the complementary case, that the agent API holding the MCP credentials refuses an unauthenticated caller."
msgstr "Dashboardet viser de indstillede servere på sin MCP-side. Det indstillede sæt er bevist på deployeringstidspunktet: deployeringskørsler ``hermes mcp test <server>`` for hver konfigureret server og mislykkes, når man ikke svarer. En vellykket kørsel rapporterer den forhandlede forbindelse og den opdagede værktøjstælling. Playwright spec dækker den supplerende sag, at agenten API At have MCP-legitimationerne nægter en ikke-autoriseret ringer."

# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "The dashboard lists the configured servers on its MCP page. The configured set is proven at deploy time: the deploy runs `hermes mcp test <server>` for every configured server and fails when one does not answer. A successful run reports the negotiated connection and the discovered tool count. The Playwright spec covers the complementary case, that the agent API holding the MCP credentials refuses an unauthenticated caller."
msgstr "Dashboardet viser de indstillede servere på sin MCP-side. Det indstillede sæt er bevist på deployeringstidspunktet: deployeringskørsler `hermes mcp test <server>` for hver konfigureret server og mislykkes, når man ikke svarer. En vellykket kørsel rapporterer den forhandlede forbindelse og den opdagede værktøjstælling. Playwright spec dækker den supplerende sag, at agenten API At have MCP-legitimationerne nægter en ikke-autoriseret ringer."

msgid "The dashboard signs in through its own public Keycloak client (PKCE S256, no service account, declared under ``services.sso.oidc.clients`` of ``web-app-dashboard``). Only that client’s access tokens carry the ``allowed-origins`` claim, which the Account REST API needs to answer the dashboard’s cross-origin calls; the shared client and the realm’s ``web-origins`` scope keep the claim out of every other access token. When an Account REST call fails, times out or answers with a non-2xx status, the dashboard still runs “Log out on this device” and, after the redirect back, tells the user that other devices may still be signed in. Neither choice uses a Keycloak service account, a ``realm-management`` role, the Keycloak admin REST API, stored realm events or a Keycloak extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "The dashboard signs in through its own public Keycloak client (PKCE S256, no service account, declared under `services.sso.oidc.clients` of `web-app-dashboard`). Only that client's access tokens carry the `allowed-origins` claim, which the Account REST API needs to answer the dashboard's cross-origin calls; the shared client and the realm's `web-origins` scope keep the claim out of every other access token. When an Account REST call fails, times out or answers with a non-2xx status, the dashboard still runs \"Log out on this device\" and, after the redirect back, tells the user that other devices may still be signed in. Neither choice uses a Keycloak service account, a `realm-management` role, the Keycloak admin REST API, stored realm events or a Keycloak extension."
msgstr ""

